---
title: Ambition is a bug
publishedAt: 2026-06-06
draft: false
---
Ambition & talent -- two of my favorite words in the English dictionary

They often go hand in hand, but at times talent can become disillusioned by the siren calls that ambition offers

The enticement of fulfilling your dreams, of being “happy” & satisfied by the proper utilisation of your talent. Wherein you pour every last drop out, squeeze yourself dry, and savour the pain

Though ambition, just like any other drug, can have a dark side, a side which can utterly ruin your life

The funny thing about this drug is that no rehab in the world can get it out of you. No matter how successful you are, ambition will keep you captive forever. Once it gets a grip on you, this demon never lets you go

It may offer temporary comfort by making you think it has been flushed out of your body & system, but the truth is, that this bug burrows deep into your brain & has two states; Active & Dormant

In the active state, this bug makes you chase your goals & revolve your life around them. This state is pretty easy to detect

On the other hand, in the dormant state, the victim does not know that it has fallen prey or rather, is still prey to this disease. It kills you from inside when you notice a colleague, a friend, or a peer doing what you gave up on

For people who chose to "settle down," this may seem very relatable. This is the reason for the resentment you feel toward the reasons or the people who made you “settle down,” who kept you from fully unlocking your potential

A classic example of this is in the case of the show *Breaking Bad*

## Walter, meth & bugs

In the show Breaking Bad, a prodigy scientist who was on the verge of creating a breakthrough company is forced to quit & resign himself to a mediocre life as a high school chemistry teacher. A job for which he is disgustingly overqualified

He has tasted both sides that ambition has to offer; the active & the dormant state

### Ambition at the forefront (Active)

Before he chose to give up on his dreams, Walter was working on his company, GrayMatter, wherein, he was focused on commercialising his scientific discoveries

As he mentions, he was having the time of his life

But one thing led to another, & he was forced to step away & settle down with his then soon-to-be wife, Skyler. He sold his shares in the company and utilized that money to pay down the deposit for a house

He chose to give up on his dreams & settle down

If ambition wasn't such a bitch, that would've been the end of the story

### Ambition on the backfoot (Dormant)

Though he chose to step away from the company & settle down as a high school chemistry teacher, he still wasn't happy with his life

He used to keep close tabs on the company & monitor the progress from afar

He grew extremely resentful of his life, of his family & the decision he took to step away. He knew he was talented & he knew he was wasting away that talent -- he despised himself for it

This led to the following feelings:

- Heightened Insecurity & Feeling of Emasculation (Family)
- Resentment & Guilt
- Latent need for recognition\

  - The funny thing about talent is that once it tastes fame, it can’t go back. Talent demands an audience, and if it doesn’t get one, it can go haywire. It can go absolutely berserk

Walter already knew he was extremely talented, but the state he was in, he did not get any recognition for his talent from his family, friends, peer or even his students. His passion for chemistry was overflowing. We, as the audience, could feel it

This actually put Walter in a very vulnerable state and was later on the catalyst for why Walter continued the line of business which he chose, despite having all the money in the world to quit

### Powdered Love

When he started cooking math, he did not realize what was about to unfold

His chemistry skills started getting the attention he craved, the kind he once got but had not tasted in a very long time

He was hailed as the best meth cook in the world -- more than money, this was the feeling that actually drove him

This actually put his ambition in the active state; in overdrive

The sirens' calls were way too overpowering for him & he submitted himself, he wanted to get lost in the trance, to feel like he was in charge once, and to feel the sweet love of being able to utilize your potential

Even in the face of life & death, he chose death

He was at peace with that choice because living without fully using your potential is a fate worse than being burned alive

## To live or not

From my perspective, it's worse than a death sentence for someone who has tasted ambition to go back to a life where they are not using their potential

The withdrawals you get when you start healing yourself from the pain are more than physical. They're more than mental. They're something subliminal

The world which we live in: ambition, obsession, power, drive. These are words which are very romanticized. The “hustle” culture

Though I believe that a person who has truly experienced obsession & ambition knows what a double-edged sword it is

Your relationships with people are not the same. Hell, your relationship with yourself is not the same

As Nietzsche puts it, "To live is to suffer, and to survive is to find meaning in the suffering.”

Maybe the cost of unlocking your potential is worth the pain?

it's for sure better than living a life of regrets. I feel bad when I find talented people who choose to live a mediocre life.

I feel pity for them

To abstain from discovering yourself is the greatest sin of all
